# DPDP Local Agent — network configuration (Phase 3B skeleton).
# Copy to agent/.env and adjust. All values are OPTIONAL; the secure defaults are
# loopback-only with an empty origin allowlist. Nothing here is a secret in 3B.

# 1) Where the agent LISTENS (bind address). Secure default: 127.0.0.1.
#    Never auto-defaults to 0.0.0.0. Set a LAN/enterprise address explicitly only
#    when you understand the exposure (see README — non-loopback mode).
# GATEWAY_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
# GATEWAY_BIND_PORT=7071

# LAN testing example (Agent on another machine, reachable from a laptop's browser):
# GATEWAY_BIND_HOST=192.168.1.50
# GATEWAY_BIND_PORT=7071

# 2) Which web ORIGINS may call the agent. EXACT origins, comma-separated. No '*'.
#    Default is empty (no browser origin allowed until configured).
# GATEWAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,https://app.your-dpdp-host.example

# 3) Extra HOST-header names browsers may use to reach the agent (DNS-rebinding
#    defense). Loopback names and GATEWAY_BIND_HOST are always accepted. Needed
#    only when the agent binds 0.0.0.0 (e.g. Docker) and browsers reach it by a
#    LAN IP or DNS name. Bare hostnames/IPs, comma-separated, no ports.
# GATEWAY_ALLOWED_HOSTS=192.168.1.50,gateway.corp.example

# 4) Where the agent reaches the DPDP workspace (enrolment, heartbeat, pairing).
# GATEWAY_CONTROL_PLANE_URL=https://control.your-dpdp-host.example

# 5) Outbound proxy for agent -> DPDP calls (see README "Outbound connections").
#    config.json "proxy" takes precedence over these. https:// destinations use
#    HTTPS_PROXY; HTTP_PROXY alone does not proxy https://. Loopback is always
#    direct, and a configured proxy is never bypassed.
# HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy.corp.example:8080
# NO_PROXY=intranet.corp.example,10.0.0.0/8
#    Corporate TLS-inspection CA for console/dev runs (read at process start).
#    For the Windows service use config.json "tls" instead.
# NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS=/path/to/corp-ca.pem
